ar8m|n|An evergreen tree (Taxus baccata) of Europe, allied to the pines, but having a peculiar berrylike fruit instead of a cone. It frequently grows in British churchyards. ; The wood of the yew. It is light red in color, compact, fine-grained, and very elastic. It is preferred to all other kinds of wood for bows and whipstocks, the best for these purposes coming from Spain. ; A bow for shooting, made of the yew.	a:524r,200,92;9fi1,163,2;2two,94,1;4rj5,45,1;2wjn,40,5;3ugs,36,6~524r,200,92;3ugs,36,6;2wjn,40,5;9fi1,163,2;4rj5,45,1;2two,94,1	v:2wjn,17,1~2wjn,17,1	V:ag3t,83,3~ag3t,83,3	n:9fi1,153,1;6tus,112,12;9k5q,23,5;598y,1,7~6tus,112,12;598y,1,7;9k5q,23,5;9fi1,153,1	N:5dta,131,1;413g,119,2;5dt8,116,2;12dt,110,3;ag3t,107,16;4133,99,5;167e,97,1;vmz,90,1;12bv,89,5;9tzf,78,10;9twb,50,7~ag3t,107,16;9tzf,78,10;9twb,50,7;12bv,89,5;4133,99,5;12dt,110,3;413g,119,2;5dt8,116,2;5dta,131,1;vmz,90,1;167e,97,1
